    <data name="atproto_settings/card_unlink" xml:space="preserve"><value>The link to {account} is removed. The external account itself is untouched — it keeps existing on its own server and is not migrated.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_mint" xml:space="preserve"><value>A new public identity {handle} is created on the Bluesky network.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_reactivate" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your identity {handle} is restored — the same identity you had before, nothing new is created.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_publish_consent" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your public posts become visible to everyone on the Bluesky network.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_deactivate" xml:space="preserve"><value>Publishing stops and the network no longer serves your profile or posts. Your identity is kept — re-enabling restores it exactly.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_no_recall" xml:space="preserve"><value>Copies of already-published posts held by other servers cannot be recalled.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_delete_pointer" xml:space="preserve"><value>"Delete my Bluesky presence" below is the separate, stronger action.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_open_plane" xml:space="preserve"><value>Third-party Bluesky apps will be able to log in as this identity once you create an app credential.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_dm_honesty" xml:space="preserve"><value>Bluesky direct messages are not end-to-end encrypted and pass through this nest in transit.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/card_suspend_plane" xml:space="preserve"><value>Connected apps stop working immediately. Nothing is deleted — your app credentials stay listed, and stepping back up restores them.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/delete_confirm_sweep" xml:space="preserve"><value>Every post published to Bluesky is deleted, and the network is told to remove them.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/delete_confirm_identity_kept" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your AT Protocol identity @{handle} is kept. This removes what you published, not who you are — turning AT Protocol hosting back on later restores the same identity.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/delete_confirm_apps_disconnected" xml:space="preserve"><value>Bluesky apps you are signed in to are disconnected, and other apps can no longer post as you.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/delete_confirm_level_off" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your Bluesky setting returns to Off.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/delete_confirm_identity_retired" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your AT Protocol identity @{handle} is also permanently retired once the deletion finishes. This cannot be undone: the identity stops existing on the Bluesky network, and no one — not you, not this nest — can ever restore it. Turning AT Protocol hosting back on later creates a new, different identity.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/delete_retire_unavailable_web" xml:space="preserve"><value>This identity is tied to your domain, so there is no separate record to retire — it ends when your domain stops serving it.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/delete_retire_unavailable_unpublished" xml:space="preserve"><value>This identity has not been published yet, so there is nothing to retire.</value></data>
    <data name="atproto_settings/error_delete_presence" xml:space="preserve"><value>Could not delete your Bluesky presence: {message}</value></data>
